<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02213</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.8.1: Evaluating Expressions Using Mental Math</md:title>
    <md:uuid>47ba50fe-27ff-4bcd-b713-add42b80969c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Finding a product mentally can be done by changing the way you look at a multiplication problem.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">For example, \(9 \cdot 39\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (40 − 1)\). For some people, this might be easier to calculate mentally since you can imagine the solution as \(360 − 9\), or 351. This method leverages the distributive property of multiplication over addition.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">For the following questions, find each product mentally. Then, explain how you changed the way you looked at each multiplication problem to use the distributive property.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">(\(9 \cdot 11\))</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">99</para>
          <para id="para-00008">For example, \(9 \cdot 11\) can be thought of as \(9 \cdot (10 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(90 + 9\), or 99.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">(\(19 \cdot 21\))</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00010">Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">399</para>
          <para id="para-00013">For example, \(19 \cdot 21\) can be thought of as \(19 \cdot (20 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(380 + 19\), or 399.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00014">(\(99 \cdot 101\))</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00015">Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">9999</para>
          <para id="para-00018">For example, \(99 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(99 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(9900 + 99\), or 9999.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00019">(\(109 \cdot 101\))</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00020">Enter the product and your explanation.</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">11,009</para>
          <para id="para-00023">For example, \(109 \cdot 101\) can be thought of as \(109 \cdot (100 + 1)\). This simplifies to \(10,900 + 109\), or 11,009.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
